WAIT_HELP = """cc4a wait — block until a usage or context threshold is crossed.

USAGE
  cc4a wait [conditions] [--any|--all] [--timeout=<dur>] [--interval=<dur>] [--json]

  Durations are <n>s / <n>m / <n>h / <n>d. Default timeout 1h; default poll 60s
  when any usage condition is present (its data is cached for 60s), else 5s.
  --timeout=0s evaluates once and exits, which turns wait into a gate.

CONDITIONS
  --usage-above=N     5h window at or above N%   (--window=seven_day for weekly)
  --usage-below=N     5h window at or below N%   (i.e. wait for a reset)
  --context-above=N   a session's context at or above N%
  --context-below=N   a session's context at or below N%

  Context conditions default to --session=<id>, or this session if that is omitted.
  To watch several sessions at once, name the target on the condition itself:

    cc4a wait --context-above=80@<id-a> --context-above=80@<id-b> --any

  which wakes on whichever crosses first. A bare --session= still sets the default
  for any condition that does not carry its own @target. Give as many conditions as
  you like. --any returns when the first is met (default), --all when every one is
  met at the same poll. They are evaluated together in one loop, so this is how you
  compose them — `cc4a wait --usage-above=90 --context-above=75 --any` does what
  two concurrent waits would, without the shell juggling. `a && b` cannot: && runs
  b only after a returns.

EXAMPLES
  Self-watch on context. Wake me before auto-compaction chooses for me, so I can
  checkpoint deliberately. A bare condition targets this session:

    cc4a wait --context-above=75 --timeout=8h --interval=30s

  Self-watch on the rate limit. Wake me when the 5h window is nearly spent, before
  I start anything expensive:

    cc4a wait --usage-above=85 --timeout=5h

  Both at once — whichever comes first. This is the useful shape for a long
  autonomous run, and is what `a && b` cannot express:

    cc4a wait --context-above=75 --usage-above=85 --any --timeout=8h

  BOTH of the above MUST be started with run_in_background. Blocking your own turn
  freezes your own context and holds your own token spend flat, so neither
  threshold can arrive; you get exit 4 after --stall-timeout instead of a hang.
  Backgrounded, your turn completes normally and Claude Code re-invokes you when
  the command exits — that wake-up is the whole point.

  Wait out a rate-limit window instead. Here nothing depends on your own turns, so
  blocking is fine — but the 600s foreground cap still means backgrounding it:

    cc4a wait --usage-below=5 --timeout=6h --interval=5m

  Orchestrator watching workers. Wake on whichever session fills first:

    cc4a wait --context-above=80@<worker-a> --context-above=80@<worker-b> --any

  Gate a script on quota, no waiting. --timeout=0s polls once and exits:

    cc4a wait --usage-below=80 --timeout=0s || echo "quota too high, deferring"

EXIT CODES
  0  condition met
  1  cannot be evaluated at all, and will not recover (no credentials, 401/403)
  2  bad invocation
  3  did not fire. Covers both "polled once, not met" (--timeout=0s) and "waited
     the full timeout, never met" — they are the same outcome, and the exit code
     does not distinguish them. The message does: "timed out after 6s" vs
     "met after 0s". For a gate, 3 is the normal negative branch, not an error.
  4  stalled: watching your own state from a call that blocks it (see below)
  5  inconclusive: a condition's data went unavailable for --unavailable-timeout
     (default 10m) and its threshold may have been crossed unobserved. NOT the
     same as "not met" — an unreadable value is not a false one.

PIPELINES EAT EXIT CODES
  `cc4a wait ... | head` reports head's status, not wait's, so a gate that did not
  fire reads as success. That is how every shell pipeline behaves — `false | head`
  is also 0 — but it fails toward "proceed", so it is worth guarding:

    set -o pipefail                        # portable; bash and zsh
    cc4a wait ... ; rc=$?                  # or just do not pipe
    cc4a wait ... | jq . ; ${PIPESTATUS[0]}   # bash    ($pipestatus[1] in zsh)

  cc4a does not warn about this itself: the reason to pipe is usually --json, and
  writing a warning into that stream would corrupt the thing you were parsing.

WATCHING YOUR OWN CONTEXT
  A session's context only advances when a turn completes. If this call is blocking
  your own turn, the turn cannot complete, your context is frozen, and the threshold
  never arrives. Run it with run_in_background and it works normally.

  cc4a does not guess how it was invoked: it watches for own-context that has not
  moved while the session still reports status "busy", and exits 4 with an
  explanation after --stall-timeout (default 5m) instead of hanging. Watching
  another session with --session=<id> has no such constraint.

LONG WAITS MUST RUN IN THE BACKGROUND
  A foreground Bash call in Claude Code is capped at 600s, so anything longer —
  notably waiting out a 5-hour window — must be started with run_in_background.
  The agent is re-invoked when the command exits, which is exactly the wakeup you
  want. Default timeout is 1h; raise it with --timeout for a full-window wait.
"""

